WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.990 --> 00:00:03.280
- [Instructor] So it looks
like we have some angry cats

00:00:03.280 --> 00:00:08.280
on our hands, meow, yeah they seem angry.

00:00:08.540 --> 00:00:11.010
And what we want to do is
think about how can we separate

00:00:11.010 --> 00:00:12.881
these angry cats 'cause the
only thing worse than an angry

00:00:12.881 --> 00:00:17.090
cat is 12 of them coordinating
potentially to take over

00:00:17.090 --> 00:00:17.960
the world.

00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:21.010
And so what we're going to
think about is can we divide

00:00:21.010 --> 00:00:25.383
these 12 angry cats into equal
groups and just for the sake

00:00:25.383 --> 00:00:29.130
of this video let's say we
want to divide them into three

00:00:29.130 --> 00:00:31.663
equal groups, how could we do that?

00:00:32.590 --> 00:00:37.580
Well let me see if I can do
it, let's see maybe this is one

00:00:37.580 --> 00:00:42.397
group right over here,
maybe this is another group

00:00:42.397 --> 00:00:47.290
right over there, and then
this is another group.

00:00:47.290 --> 00:00:49.943
Are those three equal groups?

00:00:50.970 --> 00:00:54.117
Let's see this top group has
four, this group has three,

00:00:54.117 --> 00:00:57.830
and this one has five, so no
these aren't three equal groups

00:00:57.830 --> 00:01:00.040
so that won't work it has
to be not just three groups,

00:01:00.040 --> 00:01:01.850
three equal groups.

00:01:01.850 --> 00:01:03.930
So let me see if I can
do something different.

00:01:03.930 --> 00:01:08.930
I could have these four in a
group and then I could have

00:01:09.099 --> 00:01:11.898
these four in a group, I think
this is going to work out

00:01:11.898 --> 00:01:13.480
this time.

00:01:13.480 --> 00:01:17.480
And then I could have
these four in a group.

00:01:17.480 --> 00:01:20.860
And as I just said out loud
all of these groups have

00:01:20.860 --> 00:01:23.480
exactly four angry cats in
them, which is a little bit

00:01:23.480 --> 00:01:25.740
more manageable and so this works out.

00:01:25.740 --> 00:01:28.500
I have just divided the
12 into three equal groups

00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:31.760
and how many cats are in
each of those equal groups?

00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:34.890
Well we can see very clearly
that there are four cats

00:01:34.890 --> 00:01:37.290
in each of those equal groups.

00:01:37.290 --> 00:01:40.230
So the way to think about
division in this example is you

00:01:40.230 --> 00:01:44.011
started with 12 and you
divide it into three equal,

00:01:44.011 --> 00:01:49.011
three equal groups and we
ended up with four cats,

00:01:50.034 --> 00:01:54.113
four cats per group.

00:01:55.050 --> 00:01:58.160
Now what if we wanted to
interpret it slightly differently?

00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:01.270
So let's bring in 12 more
angry cats which is always

00:02:01.270 --> 00:02:03.410
a dangerous thing to do but
we're doing it for the sake

00:02:03.410 --> 00:02:04.860
of learning so it's worth it.

00:02:06.372 --> 00:02:09.580
Meow, yep more angry cats.

00:02:09.580 --> 00:02:14.580
And now let's imagine 12
divided by three, 12 divided

00:02:14.950 --> 00:02:18.360
by three, but this time we're
not going to think about

00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:21.100
the three as three equal groups,
we're going to think about

00:02:21.100 --> 00:02:24.380
it as 12 divided into groups of three.

00:02:24.380 --> 00:02:28.200
So groups of three.

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:31.400
So each of the equal groups
will have three in them

00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:35.500
and so then that's going to
tell us how many equal groups

00:02:35.500 --> 00:02:36.933
we're able to have.

00:02:37.963 --> 00:02:40.710
So let's do that, let's
create groups of three.

00:02:40.710 --> 00:02:44.270
So let's see that's a
group of three angry cats

00:02:44.270 --> 00:02:46.010
right over there.

00:02:46.010 --> 00:02:51.010
This is another group of three
angry cats, this is a group

00:02:51.520 --> 00:02:55.550
of three angry cats and then
this is a group of three

00:02:55.550 --> 00:02:56.820
angry cats.

00:02:56.820 --> 00:03:00.200
So how many equal groups of
three angry cats do I have?

00:03:00.200 --> 00:03:05.170
Well I have one, two, three,
and four equal groups.

00:03:05.170 --> 00:03:07.940
So if I want to take 12
angry cats and divide them

00:03:07.940 --> 00:03:12.270
into groups of three I
end up with four equal,

00:03:12.270 --> 00:03:15.430
four equal groups.

00:03:15.430 --> 00:03:18.030
So there you have it, there's
two different ways that

00:03:18.030 --> 00:03:20.020
we can imagine division.

00:03:20.020 --> 00:03:23.780
And the same exact division
expression 12 divided by three,

00:03:23.780 --> 00:03:26.850
you could do it as 12 being
divided into three equal

00:03:26.850 --> 00:03:31.850
groups and then the answer
would be how many things

00:03:32.040 --> 00:03:32.873
per group.

00:03:32.873 --> 00:03:35.910
Or you could say hey 12
divided into groups of three

00:03:35.910 --> 00:03:38.893
and then that would end
up with four equal groups.

